.TH TASKHEALTH 7 "August 2026" "TaskHealth 0.2.1" "Overview"
.SH NAME
taskhealth \- Linux user-space thread health monitoring
.SH DESCRIPTION
TaskHealth is a client-daemon system for monitoring the health of threads in
Linux user-space processes, inspired by QNX HAM.  It detects three classes of
fault:
.TP
.B Unexpected exit
a registered thread terminates without cleanly unregistering.
.TP
.B Deadlock
a thread stops heartbeating while blocked on a futex.
.TP
.B Lock-wait timeout
a thread stays blocked on a futex longer than a configured threshold.
.SH COMPONENTS
.TP
.B taskhealthd (8)
The monitoring daemon: a Unix domain socket server that accepts registrations,
tracks heartbeats, probes
.IR /proc ,
and emits alerts.
.TP
.B libtaskhealth
The client library that links into business processes and sends
register/heartbeat/unregister/shutdown messages over the socket.
.TP
.B libtaskhealth_mutex
An optional companion mutex wrapper that registers mutex names so the daemon
can resolve blocked futex addresses to human-readable lock names.
.SH ARCHITECTURE
The monitoring unit is the thread (TID).  Each client process connects to the
daemon over a SOCK_SEQPACKET Unix domain socket and registers individual
threads keyed by \fI(pid, tid)\fR.  The daemon watches those threads by
reading
.I /proc
state (thread state, wchan, syscall, memory maps) and emits alerts by severity
when a fault is detected.  The client library also cooperatively reports futex
addresses before blocking, which lets the daemon resolve lock names without
ptrace.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR taskhealthd (8),
.BR taskhealth-demo (1)
